\description{
Drops the small components of a forest's functional decomposition and, optionally, refits one coefficient per
kept component. The rule and the threshold are chosen out of bag by default, from the forest itself
without refits, or by K-fold cross-validation on the training rows. By default every kept interaction of three or
more predictors is then coarsened so that it can be shown in at most \code{max_panels} heat maps, and the model is pruned
again.
}
\details{
The forest is decomposed into its purified components \code{f_S}, one per set \code{S} of predictors (see \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} and
\code{\link[=predict_components]{predict_components()}}), at all orders up to \code{max_interaction}, and its intercept \code{f_0}. The size of a component is
its share of the variance of the prediction \code{f} on the training rows, \code{share_S = Var(f_S) / Var(f)}. The shares are
descriptive sizes, not a partition: the components are not orthogonal on the data, so they need not add up to 1. A
component is kept if its share is at least \code{tau} (and it is not identically zero on the training rows). Two rules
are available:
\itemize{
\item \code{"D"} (drop): the prediction is \code{f_0} plus the kept components. \code{rule = "D"} with \code{tau = 0} is the unpruned
forest.
\item \code{"R"} (drop and refit): the prediction is \code{alpha + sum(beta_S * f_S)} over the kept components, with the
intercept \code{alpha} and the coefficients \code{beta_S} by least squares of \code{y} on the kept components (the minimum-norm
solution if the components are collinear). The components themselves are not changed, only their scale. With
\code{select_by = "oob"} (the default, see below), the least squares use each training row's out-of-bag components
instead of the forest's own, for a chosen and for a given rule and \code{tau}. The forest's own components have fitted
the training rows' noise, so least squares on them scale the components up. With \code{select_by = "cv"}, and where
the default falls back to it (see below), the least squares use the forest's own components on the training rows.
}

\strong{The choice.} If more than one pair of \code{rule} and \code{tau} is given (the default: both rules and the grid 0, 0.01\%,
0.03\%, 0.1\%, 0.3\%, 1\%, 3\%, 10\%), the pair is chosen by its out-of-bag (\code{select_by = "oob"}, the default) or
cross-validated (\code{"cv"}) RMSE. With \code{select = "1se"} (the default), the largest \code{tau} whose MSE is within one
standard error of the minimum is chosen (the SE is the standard deviation of the per-fold MSEs of the minimum,
divided by \code{sqrt(K)}), and at that \code{tau} the rule with the lower RMSE. With \code{select = "min"}, the pair with the
lowest RMSE is chosen (ties, up to a relative \code{1e-12}: the larger \code{tau}, then \code{"D"}). \code{"1se"} gives sparse models:
with \code{"R"} fitted out of bag the out-of-bag error changes little over a range of \code{tau}, and \code{"min"} keeps small
components that add nothing on new data.

\strong{Out of bag} (\code{select_by = "oob"}). Every tree family of \code{object} is grown on a bootstrap sample of the training
rows, and the purification works family by family. So every family's components (see \strong{Purification} below) are
also evaluated at the training rows outside its bootstrap sample. A row's out-of-bag (OOB) component is the mean
over its OOB families (about 37\% of them), and its OOB intercept likewise.
The purification uses the predictors of all training rows, not the outcome, so no outcome information of a row
enters its OOB components.
\itemize{
\item The kept components of every \code{tau} are those of the final model (the shares of \code{object} on the training rows).
\item \code{"D"} is scored by the OOB intercept plus the kept OOB components.
\item For \code{"R"}, \code{alpha} and \code{beta_S} are fitted as for the final model, by least squares on the OOB components, but
only on the rows outside each of K folds (\code{folds}, \code{fold_id}, \code{seed}). They are scored with the OOB components of
the fold's rows, so that no row scores coefficients fitted on it. The final model's are fitted on all rows with
at least one OOB family.
\item An OOB mean averages \code{m_i} families (about 37\% of \code{B = n_families}), the final model all \code{B}, so the OOB prediction
scatters more around its expectation. The OOB MSE is therefore corrected: per row it subtracts
\code{(1 / m_i - 1 / B) * v_i}, with \code{v_i} the across-family variance of the candidate's prediction, estimated from
the OOB means over the even and over the odd families (for a row whose OOB families are all even or all odd: the
mean estimate of the other rows). Without the correction, the noise of the OOB components makes the candidates
look worse than they are. The uncorrected values are reported as \code{oob_rmse_raw}.
\item If the correction exceeds a candidate's uncorrected MSE, its corrected MSE is reported as 0. That is not a perfect
candidate but an overshooting, unreliable estimate (it happens with very few rows, e.g. \code{"R"} with about as many
coefficients as rows, or with almost no noise). Such candidates are not chosen, unless all candidates are at 0
(then they tie).
\item The OOB RMSE is taken over the rows with at least one OOB family, the SE for \code{"1se"} from the per-fold MSEs.
\item With few families the OOB means average only one to a few families, and many rows have none (16\% at 4
families). From 10 families on, the OOB choice is usually as good as the CV's, or better where the CV's fold fits
choose poorly. Below 10 families (\code{n_families}), the default \code{select_by} scores by CV instead (with a message);
an explicit \code{select_by = "oob"} warns.
\item A deterministic forest has no out-of-bag rows, and a forest restored by \code{\link[=rpf_unmarshal]{rpf_unmarshal()}} from a blob made by an
earlier version has no in-bag counts (with a message): with the default \code{select_by}, they are scored by CV
instead.
\item A given rule \code{"R"} and \code{tau} (no choice) is fitted on the OOB components too, with the same fallbacks: below 10
families or without in-bag counts its coefficients are fitted in sample (with a message), and so they are for a
deterministic forest. A given \code{"D"} needs no OOB components.
\item Factor predictors are coded by the outcome of all training rows: their levels are ordered by the mean outcome
(regression), by the share of the second class (binary classification) or by the first principal component of
the class proportions (three or more classes; Coppersmith, Hong and Hosking 1999). So a row's own outcome enters
the coding its OOB families split on: with factors the OOB error is somewhat optimistic.
}

\strong{Cross-validation} (\code{select_by = "cv"}). For each fold, the forest is refitted with the parameters of \code{object} on
the other folds (after \code{set.seed(seed)}, the same for every fold), its components, shares and refit are computed
on those rows, and every candidate is scored on the held-out fold. The CV RMSE is \code{sqrt(sum(SSE_k) / n)}, summing
the squared errors over the K folds.

The chosen (or given) pair is then applied to \code{object} itself (not refitted): its components and shares on \code{data},
and for \code{"R"} the least squares on the OOB components of \code{data}'s rows (out of bag) or on its components on \code{data}
(by CV). \code{data} and \code{y} must therefore be the rows \code{object} was trained on, in the order of the fit (checked).

\strong{The display} (\code{max_panels}, default 10; \code{Inf} switches it off). A component of two predictors is one heat map.
A kept component \code{f_S} of three or more predictors is shown as heat maps over two of its predictors, one per cell of
the others. If its own cut points need more than \code{max_panels} such heat maps, it is coarsened:
\itemize{
\item Two predictors of \code{S} stay at full resolution. Each other predictor \code{k} is cut into \code{q_k} intervals of equal
numbers of training rows (ties merged), with \code{prod(q) <= max_panels}; \code{q_k = 1} averages \code{k} out. Only the
maximal \code{q} vectors (no \code{q_k} can grow) are considered; they are listed directly, not searched among all
\verb{max_panels^(|S| - 2)} vectors.
\item Within a cell, \code{f_S} is averaged over the coarse predictors with the product of their training marginals. The
coarsened component is not purified again: \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} identifies the components under the joint distribution of
the training rows, not under the product of the marginals, and the averaging changes the component's means along
its two full-resolution predictors and its mean over the training rows. It is used as it is (no part of it is
moved into the intercept or into components of lower order).
\item Of all choices of the two full predictors and the \code{q} vector, the one keeping the largest share of the variance
of \code{f_S} on the training rows is taken (ties: the first in lexicographic order).
}

Then the same rule prunes again, on the components the first pruning kept, with the coarsened ones in place of the
originals. Its shares, which \code{tau} thresholds, are of the variance of that model (the intercept plus these
components) on the training rows, not of the forest's prediction. A pair chosen out of bag is chosen again out of
bag, with the same candidates and folds, scored with each family's coarsened components at its out-of-bag rows (R's
coefficients out of bag); a given rule and \code{tau}, or a pair chosen by CV, is applied again as it is (R's
coefficients out of bag if the first pruning fitted them so, else in sample; a CV choice is not repeated, as it would
need the display in every fold's refit). The prediction then uses the coarsened components, so the
pruning judges the model that is shown. This second pruning is usually a little more accurate than pruning and then
coarsening, or than not coarsening. If no kept component needs coarsening, the model is the first pruning's.
The display costs about as much again as the out-of-bag pruning: a purification restricted to the components being
coarsened (two at a time) for their candidates, and one for their out-of-bag values; the stored coarsened components
take a few MB each on large data at order 3, more at higher orders.

\strong{Purification.} The pruning needs every component's values at the training rows (and out of bag, its OOB
means), not the purified forest: \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} keeps every component of every tree family as pieces, which at high
orders takes far more memory than the forest (see \strong{Cost}). So each family is
purified in turn, as \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} does (mode 2, all orders), its components are evaluated at the training rows and
added up, and the family's pieces are freed. The pruned model then stores the pieces of its kept components only,
merged over the families exactly as \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} stores them, and \code{\link[=predict.rpf_pruned]{predict()}} and
\code{\link[=predict_components]{predict_components()}} evaluate them from there; a coarsened component (the display) is stored in its coarsened
form. The components equal those of the purified forest up to rounding (about \code{1e-15} relative), and the stored
ones are the purified forest's bit for bit. \code{object} itself is not purified: an earlier \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}}, at any
\code{maxp_interaction} or \code{mode}, is left as it is and does not change the result.

\strong{Random numbers.} The folds are drawn after \code{set.seed(seed)}, and the CV's fold fits after it too. Afterwards the
random number stream is restored to its state on entry (after drawing \code{seed} if it is \code{NULL}), so the call does not
reset the caller's stream.

\strong{Cost.} The out-of-bag choice purifies the families of \code{object} once (see \strong{Purification}); it needs no fit. The
CV fits and purifies the forest K more times (each fold's forest as \code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} does). Only the components the
forest has are evaluated (at most \code{sum(choose(p, seq_len(max_interaction)))}, usually far fewer: the subsets of the
trees' predictor sets). The memory is the forest's plus about \code{n} doubles per component, three times that for the
out-of-bag choice, plus one family's components at a time per thread; storing the kept components passes over the
families once more. So the whole call of \code{rpf(n_splits = NULL, prune = TRUE)} can need much less memory than
\code{\link[=purify]{purify()}} of the forest alone, most so at high orders, where most of it goes to the coarsened components the
display keeps.
\code{nthreads} speeds up the fits and the purification.

\strong{Classification.} A classification forest is pruned the same way, with its scores: one for a binary outcome, one
per class otherwise. Every component then has a column per class, and:
\itemize{
\item A component's share sums its variance over the classes' scores, centred across the classes (as
\code{\link[=predict.rpf]{predict()}} reports them with \code{type = "link"}: the part all classes share does not count), over the
same sum for the prediction. One kept list serves all classes. (Internally \code{loss = "logit"} fits \code{K - 1} scores
against the first level; centred, the shares, the criterion and the model do not depend on which level is first.)
\item \code{"R"} fits one coefficient per kept component, shared by the classes, and one intercept per class (up to a
constant shared by the classes, which the probabilities ignore; they are reported summing to 0), out of bag as for
regression.
\item The criterion is the forest's loss's own: for \code{loss = "L1"} or \code{"L2"} the squared error of the raw scores summed
over the columns (the Brier score; least squares for \code{"R"}), for \code{"logit"} and \code{"exponential"} the log-loss of the
probabilities that \code{predict(type = "prob")} makes (maximum likelihood for \code{"R"}, by Newton's method from rule
\code{"D"}'s model). The OOB correction is its second-order form (see \code{\link[=rpf]{rpf()}}, "Classification"). The tables then have
\code{oob_loss}, \code{se_loss}, \code{oob_loss_raw} and \code{se_loss_raw} (the mean criterion) instead of the RMSE columns, and
\code{cv_loss} and \code{se_loss} for CV; \code{select = "1se"} uses the SE of the mean criterion. A binary forest with
\code{"L1"} or \code{"L2"} has one 0/1 column and is pruned as a regression forest of that response, with the RMSE columns.
\item The display coarsens a component with one form (\code{C}, \code{q}, cuts) for all score columns, chosen by the variance
kept of the centred columns; its stored form is one per score column.
\item \code{\link[=predict.rpf_pruned]{predict()}} takes \code{type} as \code{\link[=predict.rpf]{predict.rpf()}} does, and \code{\link[=predict_components]{predict_components()}} has one column
per component and class, named as for the forest (\verb{<component>__class:<level>}); with \code{"logit"} or
\code{"exponential"} each component's columns, and the intercepts, sum to 0 across the classes.
}
}
\examples{
# A main effect and a pure interaction
set.seed(1)
x <- matrix(runif(600 * 4, -1, 1), ncol = 4, dimnames = list(NULL, paste0("x", 1:4)))
y <- x[, 1] + 2 * x[, 2] * x[, 3] + rnorm(600, sd = 0.3)
fit <- rpf(x, y, max_interaction = 2, n_families = 20, n_splits = 50, prune = FALSE)

# Rule and threshold chosen out of bag (no refits): the largest tau within one SE
# of the lowest error
pruned <- prune_components(fit, x, y, seed = 1)
pruned
pruned$oob

# ... or the lowest error
prune_components(fit, x, y, select = "min", seed = 1)

# ... or by 5-fold CV
pruned_cv <- prune_components(fit, x, y, select_by = "cv", seed = 1)
pruned_cv$cv

# A given rule and threshold: no choice; R's coefficients are still fitted out of bag
prune_components(fit, x, y, rule = "R", tau = 0.01)

predict(pruned, x[1:5, ])

# A three-way interaction, shown in at most 4 heat maps
y3 <- x[, 1] + 4 * x[, 2] * x[, 3] * x[, 4] + rnorm(600, sd = 0.3)
fit3 <- rpf(x, y3, max_interaction = 3, n_families = 20, n_splits = 100, prune = FALSE)
shown <- prune_components(fit3, x, y3, max_panels = 4, seed = 1)
shown
shown$display$forms
}
